{ [%cal Ge2e4,Gd2d4,Gg1f3] White aims for an early strike in the center. In this study we'll review basic plans for less common answers to the classic Normal Variation. } 1. e4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge4d5] That's the King's Pawn Game, leading to Open or Semi-open openings. } 1... e5 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge5d4] Most of the time, Black will respond with e5, going for the Open opening family. } ( 1... c5 { [%cal Gc5d4] That's the Sicilian Defense. Black creates imbalances in the position that makes it easier to counter-attack. } 2. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3e5,Gf3d4] } 2... Nc6 { [%cal Gc6d4,Gc6e5] } ( 2... d6 { [%cal Gd6e5,Gg8f6] The Modern Variation can lead to the same following sequence. } 3. d4 ) 3. d4 { [%cal Gd4c5] That's the Open variation. } 3... cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4] } 5. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3e4] } ) ( 1... d5 { [%cal Gd5e4] That's the Scandinavian Defense. Black challenges White’s center immediately. } 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3d5] Develops a Knight and win a tempo on the Queen. } 3... Qa5 4. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd4e5] Takes the center. } 4... c6 { [%cal Ga5c7] Prepare retreat square for the Queen. } 5. Bd2 { [%cal Ga5c7,Gd2a5] Threat on the Queen that should retreat. } ) ( 1... e6 { [%cal Gd7d5] That's the French Defense. Black wants to support the pawn push to d5 next move for stable anchor in the center. } 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3e4] That's the Paulsen Variation. White develops a piece and defends the e4 pawn. } 3... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4] } 4. e5 { [%cal Ge5f6] That's the Steinitz Variation. White takes the center and gains a tempo on the Knight. } 4... Nfd7 5. f4 { [%cal Gf4e5] Gains a significant space advantage. } ) ( 1... d6 { [%cal Gf8g7,Gg7d4] That's the Pirc Defense. It's an hypermodern response where Black will apply pressure on the center from afar. } 2. d4 Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4,Gf6d5] } 3. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3e4,Gc3d5] } 3... g6 4. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3e5,Gf3d4] That's the Classical Variation, developping our second Knight to control the center. } 4... Bg7 { [%cal Gg7d4] Developping the Bishop in fianchetto, a usual hypermodern move. } 5. Be2 { [%cal Ge1g1,Ge8g8] That's the Quiet System, just going for quick castlings on King side. } ) 2. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3e5,Gf3d4] Then the King's Knight Opening is the most obvious follow-up. } 2... Nc6 { [%cal Gc6e5,Gc6d4] To which Black will most likely answer with the Normal Variation. This will allow the usual Italian, Spanish or Scotch openings. } ( 2... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4] That's the Petrov's Defense. Instead of defending their pawn, Black decides to counter attack White’s pawn. } 3. Nxe5 d6 { [%cal Gd6e5] } ( 3... Nc6 { No good way to defend our Knight, so better to exchange. } 4. Nxc6 ) ( 3... Nxe4 { We can attack the Knigth directly with the Queen. } 4. Qe2 ) 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd4e5] Pawns have been traded, we continue with normal development taking the center. } ) ( 2... d6 { [%cal Gd6e5] That's the Philidor Defense. It's a solid but somewhat passive opening for Black. } 3. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd4e5] We bring our second central pawn, generally leading to exchange variation. } 3... exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4] } 5. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3e4] } ) 3. d4 { [%cal Ge5d4,Gf3d4] That's the Scotch Game. Most of the time the Pawn is traded. } 3... exd4 ( 3... d6 { We can simply push the pawn to attack the knight and take the center. } 4. d5 ) ( 3... Nf6 { We can take the Pawn and attack the Knight. } 4. dxe5 Nxe4 5. Qe2 ) ( 3... Nxd4 { Brings to Lolli Variation. } 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. Qxd4 ) 4. Nxd4 Bc5 { That's the Classical Variation. We need to fight for our only developed piece. } ( 4... Qf6 { Other move orders can lead to the same final position. } 5. Be3 ( 5. c3 Bc5 6. Be3 ) 5... Bc5 6. c3 ) ( 4... Nf6 { [%cal Gd4c6,Ge4e5] That's the Schmidt Variation, it's the main line. We want to exchange Knights and then push the central Pawn. } 5. Nxc6 bxc6 ( 5... dxc6 { Capturing with the Queen’s Pawn would simply trade Queens and lose castling rights, with no benefits. } 6. Qxd8+ Kxd8 7. f3 { [%cal Gf3e4] } ) 6. e5 { [%cal Ge5f6] That's the Mieses Variation. We expand in the center and attack the Knight. } 6... Qe7 { [%cal Ge7e1] The Queen defends the Knight by pinning the Pawn. } ( 6... Nd5 { We can again attack the Knight with tempo. } 7. c4 { [%cal Gd5b6,Gd5e7] } ) 7. Qe2 { [%cal Ge5f6] The Queen shelters the King and unpins the Pawn. } 7... Nd5 8. c4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc8a6,Ga6e2,Gd5b6] Black can either pin the Pawn with the Bishop, threatening the Queen, or simply move the Knight to safety. } ) ( 4... Nxd4 { Taking the Knight is a mistake, allowing the Queen to develop with no immediate threats. } 5. Qxd4 Qf6 ( 5... d6 { We'll simply develop and castle long. } 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Bf4 Be7 8. O-O-O O-O ) ( 5... c5 { It’s a useless attack; we simply move the Queen to safety. } 6. Qe3 ) ( 5... Nf6 { Developing the Knight naturally is met with direct attacks. } 6. e5 ) 6. e5 Qb6 7. Qxb6 axb6 { [%cal Gb1c3,Gc1f4] We now just want to develop the Knight and Bishop, then castle long. } ) 5. Be3 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Ge3c5,Gc6d8] The Knight could capture Black’s Knight with a tempo on the Queen and win the Bishop afterward. } 5... Qf6 ( 5... Nf6 6. Nxc6 bxc6 ( 6... dxc6 7. Qxd8+ Kxd8 8. Bxc5 ) 7. Bxc5 ) 6. c3 Nge7 { [%cal Ge8h8] Black moves the Knight to prepare for castling. } 7. Bc4 { [%cal Ge1h1] We’re moving the Bishop to prepare for castling as well. } 7... O-O 8. O-O *


