1. e4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge4d5] } ( 1. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd4e5] Best move leads to the same position. } 1... c6 2. e4 ( 2. c4 { Worse case scenario we end up in the great Queen's Gambit Slav Defense. } 2... d5 { [%cal Gb1c3,Gg8f6,Gg1f3,Ge7e6] That's the Slav Defense. We support our Pawn with another Pawn, without blocking our light-square Bishop. } 3. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3d5,Gc3e4] } ( 3. Nf3 { Ends up in the same position. } 3... Nf6 4. Nc3 ) ( 3. cxd5 { We can exchange Pawns, that was the whole purpose. Tends to end in symetrical positions. } 3... cxd5 4. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3d5,Gc3e4] } 4... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4,Gf6d5] } 5. Bf4 ( 5. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3e5,Gf3d4] Would end with similar position. } 5... Nc6 { [%cal Gc6d4,Gc6e5] } 6. Bf4 Bf5 ) 5... Nc6 ( 5... Bf5 { Developing the Bishop right away ends up with a double attack. } 6. Qb3 { [%cal Gb3d5,Gb3b7] } ) 6. Nf3 ( 6. e3 { White could also close the door directly, we would still develop our Bishop. } 6... Bf5 ) 6... Bf5 ) 3... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6d5,Gf6e4] } 4. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3e5,Gf3d4] } 4... dxc4 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb7b5] That's the Slav Proper. Thanks to our Pawn in c6, the sacrificed Pawn will be harder to get back. } ( 4... Bf5 { Developing our Bishop right away would lead to a double attack. } 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 { [%cal Gb3d5,Gb3b7] } ) 5. a4 { [%cal Ga4b5,Gc8f5] Better for White to prevent b5 right away. And now we can develop our Bishop. } ( 5. e4 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gf1c4] The usual move to retrieve the Pawn can be defended and can lead to attack on the Knight. } 5... b5 6. a4 b4 ) ( 5. e3 { Similar result with a safer move. } 5... b5 6. a4 b4 ) 5... Bf5 { [%cal Ge7e6,Gf8b4] From there we'll just have to develop our second Bishop and then castle. } ) 2... d5 ) 1... c6 { [%cal Gd7d5] That's the Caro-Kann Defense. We're not directly fighting for the center, preparing d5 push. } 2. d4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd4e5] } ( 2. Nc3 { [%cal Gg1f3] } 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd5e4] } 3. Nf3 { That's the Two Knights Attack Variation. } ( 3. d4 { Returns to Classical Variation. } ) 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 { [%cal Gf8d6,Ge8g8] We want to develop our Bishop and castle. } ) ( 2. Nf3 { [%cal Gb1c3] Could lead to the same position. } 2... d5 3. Nc3 ) 2... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd5e4] We'll take this Pawn if we can. } 3. e5 { [%cal Gc8f5,Ge7e6] That's the Advanced Variation. We'll develop the Bishop first, then push the e Pawn. } ( 3. Nc3 { That's the Classical Variation. } 3... dxe4 { No good developing moves so better to exchange the Pawn. } 4. Nxe4 Bf5 { [%cal Gf5e4] } 5. Ng3 { [%cal Gg3f5] } 5... Bg6 { [%cal Gh2h4,Gh7h6,Gg1f3,Gb8d7] Pawn in h4 must be met with h6 to not trap the Bishop. Otherwise we want to develop our Knight to d7. } ) ( 3. Nd2 { Leads to the same position. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 ) ( 3. exd5 { [%cal Gg8f6,Gb8c6] That's the Exchange Variation. We'll just want to develop Knights then Bishops. } 3... cxd5 4. c4 ( 4. Bd3 Nc6 ) ( 4. Nf3 Nc6 ) 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 { [%cal Gg1f3,Gc8g4] } ) 3... Bf5 4. Nf3 ( 4. h4 { [%cal Ge7e6,Gg2g4] This can trap the Pawn if we close the door with e6 directly. } 4... h5 ) 4... e6 5. Be2 c5 { [%cal Gc1e3,Gb8d7] } *
